00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.730
Now, start thinking
about security.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:10.770
I will get to it in a second.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:14.490
We are seeing example where
within a company, two teams

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.740
going with completely
different architecture

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:19.000
with different storage solution.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.120
This was almost
unimaginable 10, 15

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.760
years, when your organization
picked one storage

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:25.920
solution or the other.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.100
Effectively, our
organization and our systems

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.200
are becoming a starfish.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.510
Starfish has this
interesting neural structure

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:36.870
where if it loses an arm,
it's going to regrow it.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:40.140
In fact, starfish can shed
an arm as a defense mechanism

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:41.680
because it knows it can regrow.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:48.460
So what about security?

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:50.870
With security, what
we're seeing is

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:53.030
we are still stuck in
the traditional model

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:56.880
of having a single watchdog team
for your entire organization.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.030
With the pace of innovation,
the velocity of changes

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:02.610
that are going on and the
sheer complexity of the systems

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:03.510
that you're running--

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:06.720
by the way, the complexity
is not going to go down.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.130
Having this relational
model is just not scalable.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.030
Our organizations
and our systems

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:17.260
have to become a starfish from
a security standpoint, as well.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:19.050
We have to emulate
the success that we

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:20.890
have created with scalability.

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:24.760
Security is the new scalability.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:28.450
So in order to effectively
disseminate security ideas,

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:30.190
patterns are a
very good mechanism

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:32.980
for you to have good
conversations with your team

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:37.139
to make intentional choices and
build security at every layer.

00:06:37.139 --> 00:06:38.680
So in the next few
slides, let's talk

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:40.900
about some of the
security patterns

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:45.940
that we commonly see
among our customers,

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:48.640
The first thing with
security is people.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:50.140
When you're thinking
of scalability,

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:52.410
we mostly think in
terms of systems.

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:55.770
But with security, people
play an equal role.

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:57.800
As I've been saying, with
a growing complexity,

00:06:57.800 --> 00:06:59.530
there are often
interesting interplay

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:02.470
between people and system.

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:04.810
So for you to have
good security,

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:06.700
there has to be
intentional architecting

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:10.400
for people and system.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:12.380
To talk about the next
couple of patterns,

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:14.640
let me set up a scenario.

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:17.270
Let's say you're a financial
company with multiple research

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:18.320
teams.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:21.200
Let's say these research teams
are operating on a common data

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:22.460
set.

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:24.540
The data set is
particularly not sensitive.

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:26.640
Let's say it's a
public data set.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.660
But the results being
produced by your research team

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:32.390
is what is providing you
the competitive advantage

00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:33.152
in the market.

00:07:33.152 --> 00:07:35.360
They are extremely sensitive
that you don't even want

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:37.514
to share among other teams.

00:07:37.514 --> 00:07:39.430
And let's say you also
have a team of analysts

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:41.526
who are looking at these
results to make decisions

00:07:41.526 --> 00:07:42.650
on behalf of your business.

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:49.450
So using that scenario, let's
talk about this first pattern.

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:51.030
This is the confused
deputy pattern.

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:53.370
This is the case when there
is an ambiguous definition

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:56.800
of responsibility or authority.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:59.040
So in our scenario,
let's say the researcher

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:00.750
found some interesting
results and want

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:03.400
to share with his team.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:06.250
He can go to our Google
Cloud Storage console

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:07.990
and hit the Share
Publicly button

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:11.590
to provide reader
access to his team.

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:15.940
But that action is not only
providing reader access

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:17.900
to his organization or his team.

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:22.480
He is providing reader access
to the entire public internet.

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:24.870
So this is not even
a bad actor scenario.

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:26.980
This is a good actor
with good intentions,

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:30.784
but still, the consequences
are not what you desire.

00:08:30.784 --> 00:08:33.200
By the way, we are revamping
this console experience, too,

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:34.000
to make it better.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:38.789
So to tactically
solve the problem,

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:41.030
the first thing you
have to take care

00:08:41.030 --> 00:08:43.600
is you have to define the roles.

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:45.840
We have predefined
roles, for example,

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:48.380
in this scenario
for the researcher.

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:50.720
Maybe you can go with
a creator role, where

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:54.520
they can create objects but not
necessarily modify permissions.

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:56.600
If you're not happy with
the predefined roles,

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.850
we have IAM custom
roles that you

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:02.600
can create with a customized
set of permissions.

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:06.675
But still, this is too
contextual, too tactical.

00:09:06.675 --> 00:09:08.300
There are a lot of
storage permissions,

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:10.460
as you can see here.

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:12.650
So is there a way to
solve this problem

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:13.990
at a higher granularity?

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:16.631
So let's talk about this next
pattern, privilege separation

00:09:16.631 --> 00:09:17.130
pattern.

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:23.990
Let's say you are using
Google KMS to encrypt data

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:26.630
in cloud storage.

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:28.340
Now you have two
sets of permissions

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:29.480
that you can operate with.

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:31.820
For example, analysts
at a higher granularity

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:35.700
can think about which
projects have which access.

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:39.860
So now no matter what the
storage permissions are,

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:44.640
people cannot read the object
without the decrypt permission.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:46.790
Let's zoom out even further.

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:50.870
If you set up a virtual private
network across your resources

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:54.620
in Google Cloud across your
buckets, objects, Google

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:58.310
Compute Engine other
services, now irrespective

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:00.350
any permission,
none of that data

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:03.070
is going to get out of that
perimeter that you have set up.

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:07.540
All right, let's switch gears.

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:10.180
Let's talk about some
encryption patterns.

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:13.030
The first one is the
key wrapping pattern.

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:17.290
This is super useful to solve
encryption problem at scale,

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:20.290
and to solve problems
like pseudo anonymization

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:24.920
for sensitive information,
such as credit card number.

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:26.450
To illustrate this
example, I would

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:30.380
like to use an example from
within our system on how

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:33.780
we use this pattern inside
of Google Cloud storage.

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:38.316
Let's say you use KMS to store
an object in our servers.

00:10:38.316 --> 00:10:40.190
When we get the object,
the first thing we do

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:41.390
is chunk them.

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:45.680
This is to make sure we use our
own infrastructure effectively.

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:49.100
The main approach here is going
to be take all of those chunks,

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:51.410
send it over to the
KMS, get it encrypted,

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:53.690
get it back, and
then store them.

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:56.090
It is pretty
ineffective, because

00:10:56.090 --> 00:10:58.070
of the data going all around.

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:00.290
And from a performance
profile, it's

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:03.590
not going to be
acceptable for you.

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:08.450
Instead, what we do is we
generate a local encryption key

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:10.550
for each one of those chunks.

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:12.680
And then after
encrypting the chunks,

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:15.590
we take those local encryption
key, send it over to KMS,

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:17.750
get it encrypted, get it
back, and then store it

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:20.735
in our system, which effectively
achieves the same purpose.

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:28.374
You could also use this example
for the [INAUDIBLE] station

00:11:28.374 --> 00:11:30.040
let's say you are
storing files in cloud

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:31.840
storage with
sensitive information,

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:35.660
such as social security
number, credit card number.

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:37.700
Instead of sending the
whole file over to KMS,

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:39.890
you could use a
local encryption key

00:11:39.890 --> 00:11:43.620
to anonymize those information
and then send that local key

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:45.557
over to KMS, get it encrypted.

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:51.400
Let's talk about this next
pattern, crypto shredding

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:53.300
pattern.

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:57.020
In order to provide the best
possible customer experience,

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:59.900
you're trying to solve
all kinds of cool problems

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:00.800
for your customers.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:03.140
Let's say you take the
data stored in your bucket,

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:05.720
move it to BigQuery
to do some analytics,

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:08.840
then take the data
further to do some more

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:11.090
analysis to provide
that additional value

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:13.200
for your customer.

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:14.910
But what is happening
is the customer

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:17.190
data is getting proliferated.

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:19.800
When the time comes
to deletion, you

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:22.920
want to effectively delete
the entire footprint

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:25.230
to maintain trust
with your customer,

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:28.920
and sometimes to meet
regulations like GDPR.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.440
So in such scenarios,
this pattern

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:32.770
could potentially be useful.

00:12:32.770 --> 00:12:34.290
Let's say you're
using Google KMS.

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:39.081
You can use the same key across
different Google services.

00:12:39.081 --> 00:12:43.120
Now, as soon as you get rid of
the key, then all of the data

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:44.380
are effectively meaningless.

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:47.260
And then you can go around
cleaning them up later.

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:49.020
But from a customer
point of view,

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:52.550
you have effectively
deleted the data.

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:55.310
We are after similar approach
within Google Cloud Storage,

00:12:55.310 --> 00:12:55.880
too.

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:58.730
The first thing we focus on is
getting rid of those encryption

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:01.580
keys to make all of
those chunks meaningless.

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:03.790
And then we go to the
garbage collection.

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:08.050
The last pattern I
want to talk about

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:10.360
is the privilege
revocation pattern.

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:12.850
In the previous pattern,
we talked about how

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:15.340
to effectively delete the data.

00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:16.890
In this pattern,
let's talk about how

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:19.690
to effectively not
delete the data.

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:21.130
I'm not joking here.

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:22.310
There are valid scenarios.

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:25.840
Let's say you're a
financial industry.

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:28.050
Then you're required
to keep some data

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:29.700
for x number of years.

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:34.740
In those cases, you can
basically revoke access

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:36.240
from a deletion standpoint.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:39.090
And then, no matter who you
are in your organization,

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:41.000
you can't delete the data.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:43.740
Google Cloud will keep it
for the x number of years

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:45.540
that you have
specified, and then it

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:49.920
will open up the access back to
you so that you can delete it.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:53.060
By the way, that image
over there is not a jail--

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:55.049
before your
imaginations run wild.

00:13:55.049 --> 00:13:56.840
That's Fort Knox, where
the gold is stored.

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:03.700
Next, I would like to invite
Subhasish to talk about the GCS

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:05.140
security primitives
that you get.

00:14:09.981 --> 00:14:11.980
SUBHASISH CHAKRABORTY:
So as Raja said, security

00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:14.680
is the new scalability.

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:17.400
Security is something which
is at the heart of our design,

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:23.100
as far as GCS in particular
and Google Cloud platform

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:24.126
in general is concerned.

00:14:24.126 --> 00:14:25.500
Security is not
something that we

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:27.833
think of something that can
be an afterthought, that you

00:14:27.833 --> 00:14:28.980
can bolt on later.

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:33.000
It is something that you
have to plan for and design

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:35.320
at every layer of your stack.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:38.290
So when our users use
Google Cloud Storage,

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:40.320
or in general when
they use Google Cloud,

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:42.240
there are lots of special
powers with regards

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:44.880
to security that they get.

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:47.590
So according to third
party estimates,

00:14:47.590 --> 00:14:50.130
Google's private
network is probably

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:52.780
the largest private network
out there in the world.

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:55.530
It has thousands of Edge nodes.

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:57.970
It has hundreds of POP nodes.

00:14:57.970 --> 00:14:59.970
As a customer, you
are never too far away

00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:03.210
from a Google point of presence.

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:05.730
Now, this is a tremendous
security advantage

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:07.290
for our customers, as well.

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:09.480
So what happens
is, say your data

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:11.200
is stored in a
particular region,

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:13.530
but you are accessing the
data from a different city

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:16.630
or you are writing to the
data from a different city.

00:15:16.630 --> 00:15:19.860
Now, with any other
cloud storage provider

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:21.660
out there, what would
happen is the data

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:24.750
would take the public
internet to the region

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:27.074
where the data is stored.

00:15:27.074 --> 00:15:29.490
With GCS, what
happens is we actually

00:15:29.490 --> 00:15:31.260
get your data through
the nearest POP.

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:32.850
So say if you're writing
from New York City,

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:35.225
we'll actually get the data
into Google's private network

00:15:35.225 --> 00:15:36.900
through the POP
in New York City.

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:39.660
And you might be actually
writing your data to Europe.

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:42.270
So this has tremendous benefits
in terms of performance.

00:15:42.270 --> 00:15:44.340
It has tremendous
benefits in terms of cost,

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:46.530
because you are not
paying egress charges

00:15:46.530 --> 00:15:48.270
for public internet travel.

00:15:48.270 --> 00:15:50.670
But most importantly, this
has also tremendous benefit

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:52.140
in terms of security.

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:53.700
Because what happens
is, your data

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:57.030
is traversing through the
private network of Google

00:15:57.030 --> 00:16:00.000
for as much time as
possible, thereby minimizing

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:04.140
exposure in public internet.

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:06.570
Now, encryption
is something which

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:10.050
is at the core of security
considerations, as well.

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:13.830
So with GCS, all your
data is always encrypted.

00:16:13.830 --> 00:16:18.390
We have multiple layers of
encryption for GCS data.

00:16:18.390 --> 00:16:20.535
Your data is encrypted
at the data layer.

00:16:20.535 --> 00:16:25.100
It is encrypted at the
storage and media layer.

00:16:25.100 --> 00:16:28.050
There are some public
cloud storage providers

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:31.920
with whom default
encryption is an option.

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:33.750
We fundamentally
don't believe in that.

00:16:33.750 --> 00:16:36.390
Personally speaking,
I find this sentence,

00:16:36.390 --> 00:16:40.080
where you have default
encryption option,

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:42.600
it seems to me an
oxymoronic statement.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:44.880
So we fundamentally
believe, as a platform,

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:48.210
that all your data should
always be encrypted.

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:49.770
Now, encryption at rest is fine.

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:51.990
What about encryption in flight?

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:55.320
All of your GCS data is
also encrypted in flight.

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:58.080
The only exceptions we have
are for multi-cloud use

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:00.210
cases, where you might be
writing to another cloud

00:17:00.210 --> 00:17:03.756
storage which is not encrypted
by default. For example,

00:17:03.756 --> 00:17:05.130
for [INAUDIBLE]
compatibility, we

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:12.150
do support unencrypted modes
of data movement in flight.

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:14.790
But for all of your
native GCS data,

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:17.260
it is always
encrypted in flight.

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:20.730
Now, in addition to encryption,
what is also very important

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:23.190
is, how do you manage
the encryption?

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:24.760
How do you actually
manage the keys

00:17:24.760 --> 00:17:26.290
which are encrypting your data?

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:27.750
So I often talk
to some customers

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:31.350
from highly regulated
industries with a high security

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:32.770
sensitivity.

00:17:32.770 --> 00:17:35.280
They say, you know, Subhasish,
everything you say is great,

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:38.130
but I cannot really store
my encryption key with you.

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:41.970
My dictates are such that I
need to store the encryption key

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:44.730
on my premises or on my HSM.

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:47.070
So GCS allows for that as well.

00:17:47.070 --> 00:17:49.650
You do not have to store
your encryption keys with us.

00:17:49.650 --> 00:17:52.290
You just supply to us during
the time you write the data

00:17:52.290 --> 00:17:55.080
or you read the data or you
do any metadata operation.

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:57.330
So we fully support
customer-supplied encryption

00:17:57.330 --> 00:17:59.340
key, as well.

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:01.860
So along with encryption,
another very key component

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:05.440
of your security
considerations is auditing.

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:08.250
For regulatory compliance,
auditing is very important.

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:10.914
For even you-- for
regulatory compliance even

00:18:10.914 --> 00:18:12.330
from your perspective,
if you have

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:15.450
to ensure things are working
the way they are supposed to,

00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:18.600
and access is the way it is,
you need to ensure that you

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:21.060
have solid audits in place.

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:23.100
So as a platform
and as a product,

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:25.650
we believe in giving
you a lot of choice.

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:28.540
So with GCS, as far as
auditing is concerned,

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:30.240
you can go for
multiple options here.

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:31.800
You can go for
Cloud Audit Logging,

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:34.760
which is a platform-wide
logging capability.

00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:36.720
So whether it is
administrative activity

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:39.060
or it is data access
audit logging,

00:18:39.060 --> 00:18:41.940
you can get it through
Cloud Audit Logging.

00:18:41.940 --> 00:18:44.160
Now, if you want
to really dig deep

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:46.140
and you want to find
out at a very fine

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:48.090
granularity that per object--

00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:50.190
what's the kind of
latency I'm getting?

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:53.280
What is the kind of data
storage patterns that I have?

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:55.745
Or how is my object
lifecycle management behaving

00:18:55.745 --> 00:18:57.330
per object level?

00:18:57.330 --> 00:18:59.700
If you are interested
in that kind of detail,

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:03.480
then you can also go for
GCS Access and Storage Logs.

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:04.965
And then finally,
from a compliance

00:19:04.965 --> 00:19:08.490
and regulatory perspective, if
you want to consume these logs

00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:13.140
and if you want to pipe them
into your pipelines, your audit

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:15.920
systems, deployment
architectures,

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:17.820
you can use
Stackdriver Monitoring,

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:20.820
which gives you programmatic
access to this audit logging

00:19:20.820 --> 00:19:23.310
information, as well.

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:26.710
So again, in many conversations
when I travel across the world

00:19:26.710 --> 00:19:29.290
and I speak to new
customers or prospects,

00:19:29.290 --> 00:19:31.770
they asked me, [INAUDIBLE]
Subhasish, all that is great,

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:33.570
but I come from
health care industry.

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:35.903
Unless you talk HIPAA-- unless
you are HIPAA compliant--

00:19:35.903 --> 00:19:37.860
I can't really use you.

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:40.250
So not only we are HIPPA
compliant, as you can see,

00:19:40.250 --> 00:19:42.930
we are a platform with
rich third-party audits

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:44.020
and certifications.

00:19:44.020 --> 00:19:45.110
We are SOC compliant.

00:19:45.110 --> 00:19:46.545
We are ISO compliant.

00:19:46.545 --> 00:19:48.540
We are PCI level
three compliant.

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:51.160
And we are FedRAMP ATO
compliant, as well.

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:53.250
So we are an
enterprise platform,

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:56.390
and we take care of the fact
that whatever level of auditing

00:19:56.390 --> 00:20:02.210
and certification you need to
be with us, it will be there.

00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:05.520
So in today's world,
PII, or Personally

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:09.080
Identifiable Information, is a
very important topic right now.

00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:11.730
It's very topical because
it's very sensitive in nature.

00:20:11.730 --> 00:20:13.380
And it's the center stone.

00:20:13.380 --> 00:20:16.090
So PII, or personally
identifiable information,

00:20:16.090 --> 00:20:19.700
is the center stone around
which a lot of regulations

00:20:19.700 --> 00:20:22.320
such as GDPR is formulated.

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:24.400
So GDPR is live
even as we speak.

00:20:24.400 --> 00:20:26.907
There are several
other regulations

00:20:26.907 --> 00:20:28.490
which are also in
the process of being

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:34.470
formulated, which all center
around, how do you handle PII?

00:20:34.470 --> 00:20:37.500
So in our platform, we have
data loss prevention APIs--

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:40.680
DLP-- which allows you
to effectively redact,

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:43.230
encrypt, pseudo-anonymize.

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:47.000
And for you to get a very
good grip on how your PII is.

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:49.590
So it allows you to
analyze your PII.

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:52.210
It allows you to
visualize your PII

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:55.560
and effectively manage your PII.

00:20:55.560 --> 00:20:58.920
So one customer use
case that I can share

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:00.610
with you is the following.

00:21:00.610 --> 00:21:03.960
So say you are a financial
organization or you are a bank

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:07.740
and you want to ingest your
data into GCS as a data lake.

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:10.500
And you want to run
big data analytics,

00:21:10.500 --> 00:21:13.530
and you want to understand
analytic patterns

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:14.580
for your data.

00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:17.400
But the challenge that you face
working with a public cloud

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:19.440
is, what do I do with my PII?

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:22.440
Because there might be so
many credit card information--

00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:25.920
first name, last name, email
ID, age, social security number,

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:27.300
and so on.

00:21:27.300 --> 00:21:29.580
So this is where DLP
comes into place.

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:31.420
You ingest your data in GCS.

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:33.050
You run DLP over it.

00:21:33.050 --> 00:21:35.160
DLP completely
pseudo-anonymizes,

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:37.819
redacts all the data.

00:21:37.819 --> 00:21:39.860
And then you can actually
go ahead with BigQuery,

00:21:39.860 --> 00:21:41.070
and you can analyze the data.

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:43.950
And when the time comes and
you want to pipe the data out

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:46.590
of the system and move
it back to, say, on prem,

00:21:46.590 --> 00:21:50.040
you can use DLP to
encrypt data as it

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:52.350
egresses the public cloud.

00:21:52.350 --> 00:21:54.840
So this allows you to
completely leverage

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:57.960
the power of Google Cloud,
and at the same time,

00:21:57.960 --> 00:22:02.820
ensure that you do not sacrifice
or compromise your PII at all.

00:22:02.820 --> 00:22:05.610
So these are all the
existing special powers

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:09.020
that Google Cloud as a platform
and cloud storage as a product

00:22:09.020 --> 00:22:10.410
gives you.

00:22:10.410 --> 00:22:13.000
But that's not all we are
here to speak about today.

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:15.600
It gives me a lot of joy to
talk about the new features

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:18.630
that we are announcing
here publicly today.

00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:21.120
For the whole last
year or so, our team

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:23.430
has been busy working
with our customers

00:22:23.430 --> 00:22:26.470
to closely understand the
problems that they face,

00:22:26.470 --> 00:22:29.700
and really solve
high-level problems so

00:22:29.700 --> 00:22:34.350
that we can continue to work
effectively in this new world.

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:36.420
The first feature I
want to announce today

00:22:36.420 --> 00:22:39.150
is access transparency with GCS.

00:22:39.150 --> 00:22:41.880
This integration is going
general availability.

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:44.820
And this should be available
for general public consumption

00:22:44.820 --> 00:22:50.190
pretty soon, maybe as soon as
this event is over-- maybe as

00:22:50.190 --> 00:22:51.540
soon as next week.

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:54.180
So with access transparency,
the main problem

00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:56.100
that we are solving
is if you want

00:22:56.100 --> 00:23:00.260
to know who it is that
has access to your data.

00:23:00.260 --> 00:23:03.810
So this "who" could be
a Googler who's SRE.

00:23:03.810 --> 00:23:08.010
And the SRE might have
access to your data

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:11.840
because, say, a hurricane
was headed towards your data

00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:12.570
center.

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:15.220
Or maybe you raised a
ticket and you said, hey,

00:23:15.220 --> 00:23:18.000
I want you to move this data
from this location to that

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:21.180
because this sets me up
for better performance.

00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:24.090
Now, from a compliance and a
regulatory perspective, what

00:23:24.090 --> 00:23:29.010
you want to have is a very
clean trail of who has accessed

00:23:29.010 --> 00:23:31.950
my data, what is the location
from which this data has been

00:23:31.950 --> 00:23:34.920
accessed, who is
this person, why

00:23:34.920 --> 00:23:36.930
has this person moved my data--

00:23:36.930 --> 00:23:38.850
what is the justification?

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:41.550
So access transparency gives
you all this information.

00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:45.135
And it is also available through
Stackdriver Monitoring for you

00:23:45.135 --> 00:23:50.320
to programmatically
integrate with your systems.

00:23:50.320 --> 00:23:54.242
The second feature that I am
announcing today is going GA.

00:23:54.242 --> 00:23:56.450
And this is, again, a feature
which will be available

00:23:56.450 --> 00:23:58.035
probably as soon as next week.

00:23:58.035 --> 00:23:59.910
We are actually in the
process of rolling out

00:23:59.910 --> 00:24:01.490
the GA changes for this.

00:24:01.490 --> 00:24:05.360
It's a bunch of security
and privacy UI enhancements.

00:24:05.360 --> 00:24:09.320
And to tell you the problem
why we're doing this--

00:24:09.320 --> 00:24:11.940
so over last year,
last year and a half,

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:14.870
across different
public cloud services,

00:24:14.870 --> 00:24:17.570
we heard from our
customers who are

00:24:17.570 --> 00:24:20.660
using some of the services that
said that one problem that they

00:24:20.660 --> 00:24:24.530
faced is, they didn't have a
very good handle on what part

00:24:24.530 --> 00:24:27.620
of their data is public and
what part of their datasets

00:24:27.620 --> 00:24:29.150
are private.

00:24:29.150 --> 00:24:32.660
So inadvertently, they ended
up over-exposing their data.

00:24:32.660 --> 00:24:36.170
And in some cases, they
were taken hostage of,

00:24:36.170 --> 00:24:39.460
because the sharing model
was not really very safe.

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:42.590
There are various solutions
with Google Cloud Platform

00:24:42.590 --> 00:24:44.660
that you don't run into
this kind of a problem.

00:24:44.660 --> 00:24:48.140
Like VPC is a major step
towards the direction.

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:50.630
But you might say,
you know what, VPC

00:24:50.630 --> 00:24:51.971
is a big sledgehammer for me.

00:24:51.971 --> 00:24:52.970
I don't want to use VPC.

00:24:52.970 --> 00:24:55.680
Can you give me
something simpler?

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:58.205
So we are doing
this UI enhancement,

00:24:58.205 --> 00:25:01.610
where in your GCS browser,
you can very clearly see

00:25:01.610 --> 00:25:04.070
which of your buckets are
public, which of your objects

00:25:04.070 --> 00:25:05.540
are public.

00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:10.330
And like the confused
deputy access pattern

00:25:10.330 --> 00:25:12.380
that Raja mentioned,
where you could

00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:15.140
be sharing the data
publicly, thinking public

00:25:15.140 --> 00:25:19.170
means your company publicly,
not public internet.

00:25:19.170 --> 00:25:21.050
So with these new
UI changes, even

00:25:21.050 --> 00:25:24.290
if you are under a wrong
impression as to what

00:25:24.290 --> 00:25:26.630
your blast radius
was, you can't really

00:25:26.630 --> 00:25:30.530
do this, because we now have
a IAM panel-based interaction.

00:25:30.530 --> 00:25:32.690
And through this IAM
panel-based interaction,

00:25:32.690 --> 00:25:35.310
you would be setting
permissions on your data sets.

00:25:35.310 --> 00:25:38.510
So there is absolutely
no way that, by accident

00:25:38.510 --> 00:25:40.190
or inadvertently,
you would be sharing

00:25:40.190 --> 00:25:43.820
this data with public internet.

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:49.170
The third feature I'm announcing
today publicly is Cloud KMS.

00:25:49.170 --> 00:25:52.160
Some of you-- some
of GCS customers

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:54.680
are actually testing
this even as we speak,

00:25:54.680 --> 00:25:57.350
because this is in public beta.

00:25:57.350 --> 00:26:00.890
What Cloud KMS
gives, to describe

00:26:00.890 --> 00:26:03.420
what is the common problem that
we hear from our customers,

00:26:03.420 --> 00:26:10.610
is they want fine granular
manageability of their keys.

00:26:10.610 --> 00:26:13.550
Like, I should be able to
create my key, delete me key,

00:26:13.550 --> 00:26:14.720
rotate my key.

00:26:14.720 --> 00:26:16.790
I should be able to
set automatic rotation

00:26:16.790 --> 00:26:17.880
schedule for my key.

00:26:17.880 --> 00:26:19.380
I should be able
to disable the key.

00:26:19.380 --> 00:26:20.580
And so on.

00:26:20.580 --> 00:26:24.050
But the main challenge they
face is hosting a key management

00:26:24.050 --> 00:26:26.090
service and assuring
that the key management

00:26:26.090 --> 00:26:29.880
service is always on, is
very highly available.

00:26:29.880 --> 00:26:31.330
It can be very challenging.

00:26:31.330 --> 00:26:33.440
Like what happens due
to hardware failure

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:34.580
or due to some error?

00:26:34.580 --> 00:26:37.610
You actually lose
an encryption key.

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:41.540
You might actually lose access
to petabytes of information.

00:26:41.540 --> 00:26:45.830
So what Cloud KMS does for
you is, with Cloud KMS,

00:26:45.830 --> 00:26:47.467
Google hosts the key for you.

00:26:47.467 --> 00:26:49.800
Google ensures that the key
is always available for you,

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:52.700
in every single region, with
the latency and availability

00:26:52.700 --> 00:26:53.970
that you want.

00:26:53.970 --> 00:26:56.060
But you still retain
all the ability

00:26:56.060 --> 00:26:58.970
to have very fine granular
management for your encryption

00:26:58.970 --> 00:27:00.140
keys.

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:03.470
And with this GCS
integration, you

00:27:03.470 --> 00:27:06.300
get the same [INAUDIBLE]
in terms of availability

00:27:06.300 --> 00:27:10.640
and durability and performance
that is there with GCS, even

00:27:10.640 --> 00:27:13.300
if you did not use Cloud KMS.

00:27:13.300 --> 00:27:16.910
Because as I said, all the data
in GCS is always encrypted.

00:27:16.910 --> 00:27:19.910
Either you use Cloud KMS and
manage the keys yourself,

00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:22.790
or Google would automatically
manage it for you.

00:27:22.790 --> 00:27:25.640
So Cloud KMS is in
public beta right now.

00:27:25.640 --> 00:27:28.230
I invite you guys to
actually test it out.

00:27:28.230 --> 00:27:32.660
And as with all other
features, Cloud KMS

00:27:32.660 --> 00:27:36.000
is also available
programmatically.

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:38.360
Now, we briefly
alluded to VPC before.

00:27:38.360 --> 00:27:42.590
VPC was a very highly requested
feature for quite a long time.

00:27:42.590 --> 00:27:46.910
A lot of customers were used
to sharing patterns and network

00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:49.520
firewalls in their
private infrastructure.

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:51.620
But the challenge with
public cloud they faced

00:27:51.620 --> 00:27:53.560
is, the moment they
come to public cloud,

00:27:53.560 --> 00:27:58.820
their existing sharing patterns
may be a little riskier.

00:27:58.820 --> 00:28:04.220
So what VPC allows you to do
is to have a network a firewall

00:28:04.220 --> 00:28:06.350
and for you to have
an environment as

00:28:06.350 --> 00:28:09.800
if you have your own private
cloud within public cloud.

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:13.310
So with VPC, you say, this
is my security access zone--

00:28:13.310 --> 00:28:15.150
this is my secure
access zone, rather.

00:28:15.150 --> 00:28:18.110
And in this secure access
zone, I put a few services.

00:28:18.110 --> 00:28:20.630
Like, you can say, these
are my GC instances.

00:28:20.630 --> 00:28:23.160
This is my GCS service.

00:28:23.160 --> 00:28:24.915
And this is my BigQuery.

00:28:24.915 --> 00:28:26.540
And these are the
services which should

00:28:26.540 --> 00:28:28.550
be able to talk to each other.

00:28:28.550 --> 00:28:31.640
By doing this, you ensure that
your data cannot be exfiltrated

00:28:31.640 --> 00:28:33.300
to any other service.

00:28:33.300 --> 00:28:36.220
Additionally, what you can
also do is, you can say,

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:37.760
these are the
on-prem IP addresses

00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:39.770
which I'm whitelisting.

00:28:39.770 --> 00:28:43.040
So this extends the
hybrid security access

00:28:43.040 --> 00:28:47.600
patterns to the public cloud
and gives you your own firewall.

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:50.230
So this is a very, very
strong security primitive.

00:28:50.230 --> 00:28:53.180
We are announcing private
beta for VPC service controls,

00:28:53.180 --> 00:28:55.210
and we invite you
to test this feature

00:28:55.210 --> 00:29:00.200
and get a chance to shape it
as it goes into production, GA.

00:29:00.200 --> 00:29:03.880
So last but not the least,
today's the first time--

00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:05.390
it gives me a lot
of joy that this

00:29:05.390 --> 00:29:06.848
is the first time
we are announcing

00:29:06.848 --> 00:29:10.980
Bucket Lock, an early-access
program for GCS customers.

00:29:10.980 --> 00:29:14.120
So what Bucket Lock is is
basically WORM storage.

00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:17.450
WORM stands for Write
Once, Read Many times.

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:19.870
It is also traditionally,
classically,

00:29:19.870 --> 00:29:22.850
called retention lock.

00:29:22.850 --> 00:29:24.580
So the use case is this.

00:29:24.580 --> 00:29:27.230
Say you are a finance company.

00:29:27.230 --> 00:29:31.970
You have to conform to FINRA,
SEC, or CFTC regulation.

00:29:31.970 --> 00:29:33.710
Or you are in movie
archive business,

00:29:33.710 --> 00:29:35.780
or you are doing general
backup and archive.

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:37.940
And you need to ensure
that all your data has

00:29:37.940 --> 00:29:39.410
a certain retention.

00:29:39.410 --> 00:29:43.620
So once I write data, say your
retention length is five years.

00:29:43.620 --> 00:29:45.470
And once you have set
that retention period,

00:29:45.470 --> 00:29:47.150
during this five
years, it should not

00:29:47.150 --> 00:29:49.070
be possible for
anybody, including me,

00:29:49.070 --> 00:29:52.740
to delete this data or
to modify this data.

00:29:52.740 --> 00:29:56.570
WORM storage is the solution
for this kind of a use case.

00:29:56.570 --> 00:30:00.740
With GCS's Bucket Lock, it
applies across all our storage

00:30:00.740 --> 00:30:01.610
classes.

00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:03.290
Unlike other cloud
services out there,

00:30:03.290 --> 00:30:05.820
it's not just for our
coldest storage tier.

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:10.010
You can use Bucket Lock across
any storage tier in GCS.

00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:12.470
And you can use the same APIs.

00:30:12.470 --> 00:30:14.330
We are just introducing
two new operations.

00:30:14.330 --> 00:30:16.620
One is set retention policy.

00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:19.570
The second is lock
the retention policy.

00:30:19.570 --> 00:30:20.240
But that's it.

00:30:20.240 --> 00:30:24.260
You can use all the
existing, new APIs.

00:30:24.260 --> 00:30:30.050
And in addition to the basic,
fundamental WORM capabilities,

00:30:30.050 --> 00:30:32.370
we also supporting
temporary hold and event

00:30:32.370 --> 00:30:34.730
hold, which are a little
more advanced features.

00:30:34.730 --> 00:30:36.980
I'll give you a couple
of small examples.

00:30:36.980 --> 00:30:42.710
Temporary hold example is, say
your company is getting audited

00:30:42.710 --> 00:30:44.767
for two weeks or three weeks.

00:30:44.767 --> 00:30:46.850
Only during that time, you
want to ensure that you

00:30:46.850 --> 00:30:48.920
have retention locks set.

00:30:48.920 --> 00:30:50.990
That is your use case
for temporary hold.

00:30:50.990 --> 00:30:53.030
An event-based
hold example would

00:30:53.030 --> 00:30:55.610
be, say you are a company who's
in the business of selling

00:30:55.610 --> 00:30:57.240
mortgages or loans.

00:30:57.240 --> 00:31:02.180
So you want to let the mortgage
period or the loan period

00:31:02.180 --> 00:31:03.020
play out.

00:31:03.020 --> 00:31:04.760
And after that, for
FINRA, you still

00:31:04.760 --> 00:31:08.610
want to hold onto that
client's mortgage.

00:31:08.610 --> 00:31:10.050
It goes for another five years.

00:31:10.050 --> 00:31:12.560
This is an example
of event-based hold.

00:31:12.560 --> 00:31:17.820
So I would invite all of you to
test out this feature, as well.

00:31:17.820 --> 00:31:21.830
And with that, I would invite
my friend Frank to give you guys

00:31:21.830 --> 00:31:23.120
a demo for these new features.

00:31:25.737 --> 00:31:27.195
FRANK NATIVIDAD:
Thanks, Subhasish.

00:31:27.195 --> 00:31:28.150
Hi, everyone.

00:31:28.150 --> 00:31:29.050
I'm Frank.

00:31:29.050 --> 00:31:31.050
I am an engineer in
developer relations

00:31:31.050 --> 00:31:34.750
that focuses on Google Cloud
Storage developer experience.

00:31:34.750 --> 00:31:38.350
Today, I'll be covering
three special powers

00:31:38.350 --> 00:31:40.690
that Google Cloud
Platform provides you.

00:31:40.690 --> 00:31:42.970
And to give this
demo context, I'd

00:31:42.970 --> 00:31:45.220
like you to take a
look at this slide.

00:31:45.220 --> 00:31:47.410
So I'm working with
this fictional financial

00:31:47.410 --> 00:31:49.072
institution.

00:31:49.072 --> 00:31:50.530
And they have a
credit card support

00:31:50.530 --> 00:31:53.280
team that has a chat support.

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:54.880
Those chat support
conversation logs

00:31:54.880 --> 00:31:57.790
are then stored into
Google Cloud Storage.

00:31:57.790 --> 00:32:00.580
So with this context,
let's jump in.

00:32:00.580 --> 00:32:02.500
Can we switch to
the demo, please?

00:32:02.500 --> 00:32:04.540
Thank you.

00:32:04.540 --> 00:32:06.890
So the first special
power I want to talk about

00:32:06.890 --> 00:32:09.000
is, let's say that you--

00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:13.570
or at least for
this demo, we want

00:32:13.570 --> 00:32:15.790
to manage the lifecycle
of the encryption keys

00:32:15.790 --> 00:32:18.430
that are used for the objects
or the conversations that

00:32:18.430 --> 00:32:19.789
are logged within this bucket.

00:32:19.789 --> 00:32:21.580
So what we're going to
take a look at first

00:32:21.580 --> 00:32:23.550
is customer-managed
encryption keys.

00:32:23.550 --> 00:32:26.500
To get started, we need
to create a new KMS key.

00:32:26.500 --> 00:32:30.840
So to do that, we
need to create a key.

00:32:30.840 --> 00:32:34.075
So I'm going to name it "credit
