00:34:37.670 --> 00:34:39.980
even if you change the
KMS key that you're using,

00:34:39.980 --> 00:34:42.170
you'll always know
which key is being

00:34:42.170 --> 00:34:45.139
used to encrypt which object.

00:34:45.139 --> 00:34:48.739
So with this special power, one
thing I would like to recall

00:34:48.739 --> 00:34:52.699
is Raja mentioning the
pattern crypto shredding.

00:34:52.699 --> 00:34:55.429
So let's say that I need to
perform a mass deletion of data

00:34:55.429 --> 00:34:57.510
within a short period of time.

00:34:57.510 --> 00:35:00.380
This, potentially,
could be a large task,

00:35:00.380 --> 00:35:02.810
which may take longer than
the period of time you have

00:35:02.810 --> 00:35:04.820
to delete this information.

00:35:04.820 --> 00:35:07.340
But now that we have
customer-managed keys,

00:35:07.340 --> 00:35:09.650
you can do this
operation by just

00:35:09.650 --> 00:35:12.365
doing a crypto shred, which
effectively destroys the KMS

00:35:12.365 --> 00:35:14.810
key that's encrypting
these objects,

00:35:14.810 --> 00:35:17.000
invalidating the
data that's in GCS.

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:19.164
So let's take a look
at how to do that.

00:35:19.164 --> 00:35:21.080
Let's go back to Security,
Cryptographic Keys.

00:35:24.440 --> 00:35:25.400
Click that.

00:35:29.250 --> 00:35:32.130
In order to do this, I'm going
to "Destroy all key version

00:35:32.130 --> 00:35:32.940
material."

00:35:32.940 --> 00:35:34.950
That will start that.

00:35:34.950 --> 00:35:37.250
And I need to type this in--
copy-paste that, Schedule

00:35:37.250 --> 00:35:38.220
Destruction.

00:35:38.220 --> 00:35:40.500
The first thing it does
is it disables the key

00:35:40.500 --> 00:35:43.972
from being used for future
encryption or decryption.

00:35:43.972 --> 00:35:45.930
And the second thing it
does is it schedules it

00:35:45.930 --> 00:35:47.160
for destruction.

00:35:47.160 --> 00:35:51.487
And it takes about 24 hours
for that key to be destroyed.

00:35:51.487 --> 00:35:52.820
Let's go back to the GCS bucket.

00:35:55.890 --> 00:36:00.780
So now that we've disabled the
key, what we can now do is--

00:36:00.780 --> 00:36:03.030
I'll show you an example,
what happens when you try

00:36:03.030 --> 00:36:05.920
to access that object again.

00:36:05.920 --> 00:36:10.360
So by accessing
this object, we see

00:36:10.360 --> 00:36:14.950
that GCS is no longer able
to decrypt the object.

00:36:14.950 --> 00:36:17.070
So we'll show this
error message whenever

00:36:17.070 --> 00:36:19.110
you try to attempt
to access the object.

00:36:19.110 --> 00:36:22.980
It's "The Cloud KMS key
is disabled or destroyed."

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:25.050
So that's the first
special power.

00:36:25.050 --> 00:36:28.410
Moving on to the
second special power.

00:36:28.410 --> 00:36:30.420
So let's say I want to
keep these conversations

00:36:30.420 --> 00:36:35.460
safe from being deleted
accidentally, or modified

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:36.910
in any way.

00:36:36.910 --> 00:36:40.120
The next thing I'm going to
talk about is Bucket Lock.

00:36:40.120 --> 00:36:42.570
I'm going to cover a brief
preview of the UI changes

00:36:42.570 --> 00:36:45.660
that are coming to the storage
browser in order for you

00:36:45.660 --> 00:36:48.090
to set a retention
policy for your bucket.

00:36:48.090 --> 00:36:51.090
As you can see, there
will be a new tab

00:36:51.090 --> 00:36:52.530
for the retention policy.

00:36:52.530 --> 00:36:55.410
And I can now set
a retention policy

00:36:55.410 --> 00:36:57.945
within certain
different units of time.

00:36:57.945 --> 00:37:00.070
I'm going to use seconds
for the sake of this demo,

00:37:00.070 --> 00:37:01.290
and set it to 10 seconds.

00:37:01.290 --> 00:37:03.370
Click Save.

00:37:03.370 --> 00:37:05.760
Two new lines come up.

00:37:05.760 --> 00:37:08.260
The lock state is unlocked, so
I can still modify and remove

00:37:08.260 --> 00:37:09.670
this retention policy.

00:37:09.670 --> 00:37:12.820
And the effective time is
when this policy took effect.

00:37:12.820 --> 00:37:14.377
Let's go over, back
to the objects,

00:37:14.377 --> 00:37:16.210
and I'll try to upload
the same conversation

00:37:16.210 --> 00:37:17.130
that we did before.

00:37:20.022 --> 00:37:21.210
Close that.

00:37:21.210 --> 00:37:24.830
And I will try to delete it
before 10 seconds are up.

00:37:24.830 --> 00:37:26.720
All right, cool.

00:37:26.720 --> 00:37:29.000
So as you can see
here, it's forbidden,

00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:31.784
that I cannot delete
this object at this time.

00:37:31.784 --> 00:37:33.950
So Bucket Lock has removed
the necessary permissions

00:37:33.950 --> 00:37:36.620
that I need in order
to delete this object.

00:37:36.620 --> 00:37:38.660
After 10 seconds,
or the time frame

00:37:38.660 --> 00:37:40.580
that you've set in
this retention policy,

00:37:40.580 --> 00:37:42.470
you should be able to delete it.

00:37:42.470 --> 00:37:44.210
And you can automate
this process

00:37:44.210 --> 00:37:48.680
with Object Lifecycle
Management if you wish to do so.

00:37:48.680 --> 00:37:50.900
And then the last
thing I want to cover

00:37:50.900 --> 00:37:55.880
is the last special power.

00:37:55.880 --> 00:37:58.460
So these conversations that
are held with this credit card

00:37:58.460 --> 00:38:01.775
support team, they need
to verify the customer

00:38:01.775 --> 00:38:02.900
that they're speaking with.

00:38:02.900 --> 00:38:05.390
So there's most likely
PII in this conversation.

00:38:05.390 --> 00:38:07.130
But for all intents
and purposes,

00:38:07.130 --> 00:38:08.440
you're most likely going to--

00:38:08.440 --> 00:38:11.960
or at least, we're just going
to use it for quality assurance,

00:38:11.960 --> 00:38:13.640
just to make sure
the customer's happy.

00:38:13.640 --> 00:38:15.500
And we don't need
that PII to verify

00:38:15.500 --> 00:38:17.000
that the customer is happy.

00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:20.570
So I'm going to
cover a DLP demo.

00:38:20.570 --> 00:38:25.710
So let's take a look at this,
the short little conversation

00:38:25.710 --> 00:38:26.210
here.

00:38:26.210 --> 00:38:29.750
As you can see, it's between a
customer and a customer support

00:38:29.750 --> 00:38:30.680
team member.

00:38:30.680 --> 00:38:32.780
As I scroll down
some more, you'll

00:38:32.780 --> 00:38:35.930
notice that a lot more PII
was provided than necessary

00:38:35.930 --> 00:38:40.500
to verify this customer.

00:38:40.500 --> 00:38:43.520
So we'd like to use a
data loss prevention

00:38:43.520 --> 00:38:46.110
API to go ahead and
redact this information.

00:38:46.110 --> 00:38:47.804
So I'm going to
click End Chat, which

00:38:47.804 --> 00:38:48.970
will then start the process.

00:38:53.210 --> 00:38:54.362
That happens, sometimes.

00:38:54.362 --> 00:38:55.320
So I have this new one.

00:38:58.190 --> 00:38:59.810
So you can see,
over to your right,

00:38:59.810 --> 00:39:02.900
it has redacted the PII
from the conversation, which

00:39:02.900 --> 00:39:04.910
then can be stored in GCS.

00:39:04.910 --> 00:39:08.250
As you can see, it does
it for images, as well.

