00:40:36.130 --> 00:40:38.590
So with that, just
a quick background

00:40:38.590 --> 00:40:41.000
in terms of what
Cohesity's about.

00:40:41.000 --> 00:40:45.610
So Cohesity is a
technology startup

00:40:45.610 --> 00:40:48.700
right here in Silicon
Valley, and with the mission

00:40:48.700 --> 00:40:52.740
of redefining what we
call a secondary storage.

00:40:52.740 --> 00:40:55.980
So if you look at
just data in general,

00:40:55.980 --> 00:41:00.040
you have, obviously, your
mission-critical primary

00:41:00.040 --> 00:41:01.970
environments.

00:41:01.970 --> 00:41:06.010
But what we see is, there is
a lot more growth in what we

00:41:06.010 --> 00:41:07.450
refer to as secondary storage.

00:41:07.450 --> 00:41:13.240
And that refers to backup,
test dev, disaster recovery,

00:41:13.240 --> 00:41:16.870
because of the fact that
for every primary dataset,

00:41:16.870 --> 00:41:20.500
there are multiple copies of
that data that exists today

00:41:20.500 --> 00:41:23.180
with the incumbent solutions.

00:41:23.180 --> 00:41:26.950
So the first challenge
that we have with customers

00:41:26.950 --> 00:41:29.030
is in the aspect of
backup and recovery,

00:41:29.030 --> 00:41:31.240
which is referred to
as data protection.

00:41:31.240 --> 00:41:33.550
That's where the Cohesity-Google
partnership really

00:41:33.550 --> 00:41:35.000
comes into play.

00:41:35.000 --> 00:41:37.560
So what you see here
is, on the left,

00:41:37.560 --> 00:41:41.570
we have the Cohesity data
platform, as we call it,

00:41:41.570 --> 00:41:46.390
which is a scale-out on-premise
solution, in this case,

00:41:46.390 --> 00:41:49.960
that can integrate seamlessly
with Google Cloud Storage--

00:41:49.960 --> 00:41:52.900
with any class of Google Cloud
Storage, as a matter of fact,

00:41:52.900 --> 00:41:56.130
whether it's regional,
multi-regional, nearline,

00:41:56.130 --> 00:41:58.250
and coldline, as well.

00:41:58.250 --> 00:42:01.090
So the idea here is,
with the data platform

00:42:01.090 --> 00:42:04.280
that we have on
premise, customers

00:42:04.280 --> 00:42:07.850
can then easily set policies.

00:42:07.850 --> 00:42:11.470
And again, everything that we
do is policy-based, API-driven

00:42:11.470 --> 00:42:12.610
architecture.

00:42:12.610 --> 00:42:17.050
So customers can set policies
to manage their backups,

00:42:17.050 --> 00:42:22.350
retention policies, and
then extend it to leverage

00:42:22.350 --> 00:42:27.140
the GCS offering for
really long-term retention.

00:42:27.140 --> 00:42:30.470
So what you see here
is, in this example,

00:42:30.470 --> 00:42:34.570
customers typically back
up the data on premise.

00:42:34.570 --> 00:42:36.940
And then the policies
will seamlessly

00:42:36.940 --> 00:42:39.890
move that data into GCS.

00:42:39.890 --> 00:42:40.390
and.

00:42:40.390 --> 00:42:45.160
In this case, Cohesity manages
that retention both on premise

00:42:45.160 --> 00:42:50.380
as well as in Google Cloud.

00:42:50.380 --> 00:42:53.170
To extend this a
little bit, we also

00:42:53.170 --> 00:42:57.870
have an offering which we can
run within the GCP platform.

00:42:57.870 --> 00:43:02.110
And we call that the Cohesity
Data Platform Cloud Edition.

00:43:02.110 --> 00:43:06.340
So like Subhasish
alluded to, more and more

00:43:06.340 --> 00:43:10.460
workloads are also born
in the cloud these days.

00:43:10.460 --> 00:43:13.270
And so backup data recovery
and data protection

00:43:13.270 --> 00:43:17.320
becomes a critical aspect of it,
not only for your on premise,

00:43:17.320 --> 00:43:20.930
but also within the
workloads running in GCP.

00:43:20.930 --> 00:43:23.960
So that's where Cloud
Edition comes into play.

00:43:23.960 --> 00:43:26.560
So in a similar
vein, you can now

00:43:26.560 --> 00:43:31.120
set up policies to back up
your infrastructure running

00:43:31.120 --> 00:43:33.960
in GCP onto the Cloud
Edition platform.

00:43:33.960 --> 00:43:37.930
And that Cloud
Edition actually runs

00:43:37.930 --> 00:43:41.500
on the compute
infrastructure, GCE.

00:43:41.500 --> 00:43:45.010
It uses persistent disks.

00:43:45.010 --> 00:43:51.210
And then GCS can be an extension
of those persistent disks that

00:43:51.210 --> 00:43:54.100
are there in the Google Cloud.

00:43:54.100 --> 00:43:57.800
The benefits to
customers in general,

00:43:57.800 --> 00:44:00.230
I would say broadly
three things.

00:44:00.230 --> 00:44:03.880
One is the simplicity
and ease of use

00:44:03.880 --> 00:44:07.690
that we bring to the table in
terms of backup and recovery.

00:44:07.690 --> 00:44:10.040
The second is the
scalability of the solutions.

00:44:10.040 --> 00:44:13.840
So obviously, when we are
talking the scale of GCP,

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:20.332
then the offering that we have
also needs to be scalable.

00:44:20.332 --> 00:44:21.790
So that's the second
aspect of it--

00:44:21.790 --> 00:44:24.460
the fact that we can scale
both on premise as well

00:44:24.460 --> 00:44:30.370
as in the cloud with more
of a pay-as-you-grow model.

00:44:30.370 --> 00:44:33.700
As your needs grow, you can
keep adding more and more

00:44:33.700 --> 00:44:35.770
of the Cohesity solution.

00:44:35.770 --> 00:44:39.340
And last but not
least is the security.

00:44:39.340 --> 00:44:43.150
The fact that we can
leverage the encryption

00:44:43.150 --> 00:44:46.610
that Google offers, and then
when it's on premise, the data

00:44:46.610 --> 00:44:51.470
is again encrypted,
and in flight as well.

00:44:51.470 --> 00:44:54.820
And then tying it in
with the VPC elements

00:44:54.820 --> 00:44:56.890
that Subhasish brought up.

00:44:56.890 --> 00:44:58.600
So in order to do
that, let me actually

00:44:58.600 --> 00:45:02.220
illustrate that with a real
customer example with one

00:45:02.220 --> 00:45:04.330
