00:45:39.720 --> 00:45:42.570
And in fact, they
have grown that

00:45:42.570 --> 00:45:44.970
over the last several months.

00:45:44.970 --> 00:45:47.170
And then for the
long-term retention,

00:45:47.170 --> 00:45:50.250
send the data on a
policy-based schedule

00:45:50.250 --> 00:45:53.460
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:45:53.460 --> 00:45:56.460
So that's where
they started with.

00:45:56.460 --> 00:46:01.290
As an extension for their
workloads running within GCP,

00:46:01.290 --> 00:46:06.090
they are now using our
data platform Cloud Edition

00:46:06.090 --> 00:46:08.740
in a VPC that's been defined.

00:46:08.740 --> 00:46:12.750
And the reason they leverage
the VPC and IAM concepts

00:46:12.750 --> 00:46:17.220
that Raja talked about is they
have several different projects

00:46:17.220 --> 00:46:18.480
within GCP--

00:46:18.480 --> 00:46:23.340
basically, various different
teams, and also acquisitions

00:46:23.340 --> 00:46:25.170
that have happened
over time, resulting

00:46:25.170 --> 00:46:27.510
in a number of GCP projects.

00:46:27.510 --> 00:46:32.910
So they manage all this
through the VPC and IAM roles.

00:46:32.910 --> 00:46:35.010
And in the backup
recovery context,

00:46:35.010 --> 00:46:37.000
IAM roles are even
more significant,

00:46:37.000 --> 00:46:43.470
because in terms of managing
just the administrative tasks,

00:46:43.470 --> 00:46:45.290
you can have one role.

00:46:45.290 --> 00:46:48.390
But in order to provide
self-service capability back

00:46:48.390 --> 00:46:52.140
to the end users, you want
them to have the ability

00:46:52.140 --> 00:46:55.180
to recover their virtual
machines, their files.

00:46:55.180 --> 00:46:57.690
And so there are
various different roles

00:46:57.690 --> 00:47:01.630
created in order to facilitate
self-service recovery, as well.

00:47:01.630 --> 00:47:05.010
So again, it ties in back
to the VPC and IAM security

00:47:05.010 --> 00:47:06.960
constructs.

00:47:06.960 --> 00:47:10.670
And the last element
